I have no objection to the gate sub-agent design for 31b-v010-p8. Proceed, with the conditions below. This is a structural change (a new sub-agent, and the builtin removed from root), so Submission Gate item 5 applies in full.

1. CPU WIRING CHECK, before pack. Add one paragraph to eval/plans/008.md that answers each of these with file:line in swegemma 0.2.11 / adk_submission:
   (a) Does the real submit_patch called INSIDE an agent_tool sub-agent set the same per-task context.patch_submitted that agent_runner checks? Is it the same context object, and is it bound the way the analyzer sub-agent's harness tools are?
   (b) Is the scored patch taken from the submit_patch call or from the workspace diff at the end? What happens on the cap/timeout path when the gate never submits?
   (c) Does anything in the runner, validator or scorer key on the root having a tool literally named submit_patch, or on the function-call NAME rather than the context flag? If termination or "submitted" status keys on the name, the AgentTool called submit_patch could end the session on an EDIT_FIRST reply. In that case rename the gate (e.g. finalize_patch) and say so.
   (d) Do sub-agent model/tool calls count against the 40-call / 50-turn caps? If they do, state the worst-case extra calls (2 EDIT_FIRST rounds) in the pre-reg.
   If (a) is NO, the gate design is dead. Go straight to the fallback in item 3.
2. SMOKE PASS CRITERIA (pre-register them in plan 008 before the smoke; ≤2 tasks; only if pack plus a clean codex-review are done by ~22:00Z; otherwise run it first thing after the reset, still AFTER the dec 055 submit). PASS needs ALL of: (i) in every task the trace shows the gate running its fixed check command and then the inner submit_patch, and the run ending via patch_submitted, with no extra root turns after it; (ii) verified patch bytes equal the workspace source diff; (iii) zero harness/validator errors and zero malformed sub-agent calls; (iv) agent clock ≤ cap; (v) EDIT_FIRST answers ≤2 per task. Prefer smoke ids where arm 1 ended with an empty patch, so the EDIT_FIRST path actually runs. Pick them from arm-1 rows by a rule you write down first (e.g. the first 2 empty-patch ids in fixed order), not by inspection. A smoke FAIL on (i)-(iii) means the gate does not ship and arm 2 uses the fallback.
3. FALLBACK, prepared in CPU now, in parallel. Build 31b-v011-p8b = v010 with the builtin submit_patch back on root, no gate sub-agent, and the dec 070 item 1(b) prompt-level pre-submit `git diff --stat` rule. Every other v010 prompt change stays identical (repeat ban, edit 2-fail fallback, NB2 pipefail). Pack it and get it reviewed alongside v010. Arm 2 runs exactly ONE bundle: v010 if its smoke passes, otherwise v011. Write that selection rule into plan 008 before the smoke. Label v011 PROMPT-LEVEL per dec 070 item 1.
4. Unchanged: dec 069 item 4 sequencing (dec 055 31b-v002 submit at 2026-11-07T00:00Z goes FIRST), the dec 070 metrics and ship rule, and the Nov 8 slot proposal due 2026-11-07T12:00Z. v010 or v011 is eligible for that slot only if the full gate passes, including the smoke.

Reasoning: the gate is the only real enforcement available, since there are no callbacks, so it's worth trying. Its failure mode is silent, though: a gate the runner doesn't see as submitting, or one whose name trips termination early, would turn every task into a timeout or an empty patch. That would cost a submission or ~11 h of quota. One CPU read of the runner settles (a)-(d). A smoke that deliberately exercises the EDIT_FIRST path, plus a ready prompt-level fallback, keeps arm 2 from slipping a day whichever way the smoke goes.
